    p.Article-Paragraph.
      In previous lessons we has defined the #[+Keyword--YDID only] #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID one] #[+Term--YDID route]
        to get some feedback keeping the application as simple as possible.
      This #[+Term--YDID route] has #[+ImportantEntity--YDID GET] #[+Term--YDID HTTP method] and #[+Term--YDID path]
        #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID /]:

    +CodeViewer--YDF
      +CodeViewer-Listing--YDF({ codeLanguage: CodeViewer__YDF.SupportedCodesLanguages.TypeScript }): :code_listing--yda
        import {
          Server,
          Request,
          Response,
          ProtocolDependentDefaultPorts,
          HTTP_Methods
        } from "@yamato-daiwa/backend";


        Server.initializeAndStart({
          IP_Address: "127.0.0.1",
          HTTP: { port: ProtocolDependentDefaultPorts.HTTP },
          routing: [
            {
              HTTP_Method: HTTP_Methods.get,
              pathTemplate: "/",
              async handler(request: Request, response: Response): Promise<void> {
                return response.submitWithSuccess({ HTML_Content: "<h1>Hello, world!</h1>" });      }
            }
          ]
        });

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      The #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID routing] #[+Term--YDID property] is the #[+Keyword--YDID array], so to
        define other #[+Term--YDID routes], it is required to add the new #[+Term--YDID elements] by the same way:

    +CodeViewer--YDF
      +CodeViewer-Listing--YDF({ codeLanguage: CodeViewer__YDF.SupportedCodesLanguages.TypeScript }): :code_listing--yda
        import {
          Server,
          Request,
          Response,
          ProtocolDependentDefaultPorts,
          HTTP_Methods
        } from "@yamato-daiwa/backend";


        Server.initializeAndStart({
          IP_Address: "127.0.0.1",
          HTTP: { port: ProtocolDependentDefaultPorts.HTTP },
          routing: [

            {
              HTTP_Method: HTTP_Methods.get,
              pathTemplate: "/",
              async handler(_request: Request, response: Response): Promise<void> {
                return response.submitWithSuccess({
                  HTML_Content: "<h1>Top Page</h1>"
                });
              }
            },

            {
              HTTP_Method: HTTP_Methods.get,
              pathTemplate: "/products",
              async handler(_request: Request, response: Response): Promise<void> {
                return response.submitWithSuccess({
                  HTML_Content: "<h1>Products</h1>"
                });
              }
            },

            {
              HTTP_Method: HTTP_Methods.get,
              pathTemplate: "/checkout",
              async handler(_request: Request, response: Response): Promise<void> {
                return response.submitWithSuccess({
                  HTML_Content: "<h1>Checkout</h1>"
                });
              }
            }

          ]
        });

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      As you see, #[+Keyword--YDID each] #[+Term--YDID element] of #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID routing]
        #[+Term--YDID array] in the example above is the #[+Term--YDID object] with #[+Keyword--YDID three]
          #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID required] #[+Term--YDID properties]: #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID HTTP_Method],
          #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID pathTemplate] and #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID handler].
      But why the second one called #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID "pathTemplate"], not&nbsp;just "path"?
      Because commonly the #[+Term--YDID path] could include the #[+Term--YDID parameters], but since their values
      are #[+Keyphrase--YDID #[+Keyword--YDID unknown] at advance], they must be specified according to the covention.


    //- ─── Defining of Routes with Path Parameters ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    +Heading--YDF({
      level: 3,
      text: localizedSectioning.routingInYDB.$children.pathParameters.$heading,
      HTML_ID: localizedSectioning.routingInYDB.$children.pathParameters.$anchor
    }).Article-Heading3

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      Any popular framework for the server-side development could detect the values of the #[+Term--YDID path parameters]
        in #[+ImportantEntity--YDID URI] (for example, the product identifier #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID 1] in
        #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID https://example.com/products/1]).
      However, there is #[+Keyword--YDID no]&nbsp;many frameworks which could do it #[+Term--YDID type-safely].
      To be fair, it is hardly possible to do it with 100% #[+Term--YDID type safety] in the
        #[+ImportantEntity--YDID TypeScript] — we will consider below why.
      Nevertheless, it is possible not&nbsp;only to avoid the usage of #[+Keyword--YDID detrimental]
        #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID any] type, but also corroborate the casting of more wide types to more narrow
        ones by the #[+Term--YDID validation].

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      To have something to compare it to, let us consider the case with #[+SecondaryEntity--YDID Express.js].
      There, the #[+Term--YDID route] #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID https://example.com/products/{ID}] is been defined
        as below.

    +CodeViewer--YDF
      +CodeViewer-Listing--YDF({ codeLanguage: CodeViewer__YDF.SupportedCodesLanguages.TypeScript }): :code_listing--yda
        import type { Express as ExpressApplication } from "express";
        import type Express from "express";
        import createExpressApplication from "express";


        const expressApplication: ExpressApplication = createExpressApplication();

        expressApplication.get(
          "/products/:ID",
          (request: Express.Request<{ ID: string; }>, response: Express.Response): void => {
            response.send(`<h1>Product with ID: ${ request.params.ID }</h1>`);
          }
        );

        expressApplication.listen(80, "127.0.0.1");

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      As can be seen the #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID Express.Request] #[+Term--YDID type] is the
        #[+Term--YDID generic] where the #[+Keyword--YDID first] #[+Term--YDID parameter] is the
        #[+Term--YDID object] which #[+Keyword--YDID must] satisfy to
        #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID { &lsqb;key: string&rsqb;: string; }] #[+Term--YDID constraint].
      What kind of problems are there?

    dl.Article-DescriptionList

      dt Low lever of type safety
      dd.
        The #[+Term--YDID casting] of #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID { &lsqb;key: string&rsqb;: string; }]
          #[+Term--YDID type] to #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID { ID: string; }] is #[+Keyword--YDID unsubstantiated].
        Even though we have the #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID "/products/:ID"] #[+Term--YDID path]
          #[+Keyword--YDID template], we can specify completely unrelated #[+Term--YDID type]
          (for example, #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID { foo: string; bar: string; }]) and
          #[+ImportantEntity--YDID TypeScript] will #[+Keyword--YDID not] suspect #[+Keyword--YDID anything].
        #[+Keyword--YDID Fundamentally] this problem can&nbsp;#[+Keyword--YDID not]&nbsp;be solved because the
          #[+Term--YDID type] annotation #[+Keyphrase--YDID ceases to exist] during the the #[+Term--YDID transpiling]
          from #[+ImportantEntity--YDID TypeScript] to #[+ImportantEntity--YDID JavaScript], thus when executing the
          #[+Term--YDID JavaScript code] by the #[+Term__Contrast--YDID Node.js server] the specified type
          can&nbsp;#[+Keyword--YDID not]&nbsp;be referred to check does the actual
          #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID request.params] #[+Term--YDID object] corresponding to specified
          #[+Term--YDID type].
        However, the #[+Term--YDID casting] of #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID { &lsqb;key: string&rsqb;: string; }] to
         #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID { ID: string; }] must be reinforced by something, and it is primarily
          a #[+Term--YDID validation].
        The #[+SecondaryEntity--YDID Express.js] does&nbsp;#[+Keyword--YDID not]&nbsp;suggest such functionality,
          but there are the third-party solutions like
          #[+Link--YDF({ unendorsedExternalURI: "https://express-validator.github.io/docs" }).Article-Link express-validator].

      dt Uncomfortable API
      dd
        | The #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID Express.Request] has whole #[+ImportantEntity--YDID 5]
        |   #[+Term--YDID generic parameters], herewith if we need, for example, the forth one (the
        |   #[+Term--YDID query parameters] represented as the #[+Term--YDID object]), it will required to specify the
        |   previous three ones ever they are not actual for use, so we will have something like
        |   #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID Express.Request&lt;{}, {}, {}, { pageNumber: number; }&gt;].
        |
        +Link--YDF({
          unendorsedExternalURI: sharedExternalLinks.ESLint.plugins.typeScript.top
        }).Article-Link typescript-eslint
        |   can
        |
        +Link--YDF({
          unendorsedExternalURI: sharedExternalLinks.ESLint.plugins.typeScript.rules.noEmptyObjectType
        }).Article-Link complain
        |   about such code.

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      In #[+ImportantEntity--YDID YDB], #[+Keyword--YDID both] problems has solution out of box.

    +AdmonitionBlock--YDF({
      title: "Careful with Terminology",
      SVG_Icon: true,
      geometricVariation: AdmonitionBlock__YDF.GeometricVariations.stickyNoteLike,
      decorativeVariation: AdmonitionBlock__YDF.DecorativeVariations.warning
    })

      p.Article-Paragraph.
        The #[+Term--YDID "parameter"] term is too wide, bur from now #[+Keyword--YDID three] kinds of
          #[+Term--YDID parameters] will be mentioned.

      dl.Article-DescriptionList

        dt Function / Method Parameters
        dd.
          Usually this kind of #[+Term--YDID parameters] is been associated first when "parameter" work has been said
            (in programming context).
          However, this term is being used not&nbsp;only in relation to #[+Term--YDID functions] and
            #[+Term--YDID methods].

        dt Generic Parameters
        dd.
          Actual for the programming languages with #[+Term--YDID static typing], which
            #[+ImportantEntity--YDID TypeScript] can be called, even a stretch.

        dt Route Parameters
        dd.
          Related with the #[+Term--YDID routing] concept, but #[+Keyword--YDID not]&nbsp;with specific
            programming language.

      p.Article-Paragraph.
        This terminology is #[+Term--YDID common] and does&nbsp;#[+Keyword--YDID not]&nbsp;related with specifically
          with #[+ImportantEntity--YDID YDB], but the programmes with low experience can be confused.

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID request] #[+Term--YDID object] itself (of course, it has #[+Keyword--YDID different]
        #[+Term--YDID type] with #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID Express.Request] one from
        #[+SecondaryEntity--YDID Express.js] framework)
        has&nbsp;#[+Keyword--YDID no]&nbsp;#[+Term--YDID generic parameters].
      To access to #[+Term--YDID route parameters], invoke
        #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID validateAndProcessRoutePathParameters] #[+Term--YDID method] of
        #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID request] #[+Term--YDID object], herewith:

    ul.Article-UnorderedList

      li.
        It if #[+Keyword--YDID required] to specify the #[+Term--YDID generic parameter] (once again:
          when calling #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID validateAndProcessRoutePathParameters]
          #[+Term--YDID method], #[+Keyword--YDID not]&nbsp;when annotating the #[+Term--YDID type] of
          #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID request]), the #[+Term--YDID object-type] representing the
          #[+Term--YDID route path parameters].
        #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID validateAndProcessRoutePathParameters] #[+Term--YDID method] has
        #[+Keyword--YDID only] #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID one] #[+Term--YDID generic parameter].

      li
        | The #[+Term--YDID object] validation rules must be passed as #[+Keyword--YDID sole] #[+Term--YDID parameter] in
        |
        +Link--YDF({
          endorsedExternalURI: sharedExternalLinks.YamatoDaiwaES_Extensions.RawObjectDataProcessor.PropertiesSpecification
        }) RawObjectDataProcessor
        |
        | format from the
        |
        +Link--YDF({
          endorsedExternalURI: sharedExternalLinks.YamatoDaiwaES_Extensions.top,
        }) @yamato-daiwa/es-extensions
        |   library.
        | In this case we are specifying that the #[+Term--YDID route parameter] #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID PRODUCT_ID]
        |   must be required (in "#[+Keyword--YDID neither]&nbsp;#[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID undefined]
        |   #[+Keyword--YDID nor]&nbsp;#[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID null] meaning) #[+Term--YDID string]:

    +CodeViewer--YDF
      +CodeViewer-Listing--YDF({ codeLanguage: CodeViewer__YDF.SupportedCodesLanguages.TypeScript }): :code_listing--yda
        import { Server, Request, Response, ProtocolDependentDefaultPorts, HTTP_Methods } from "@yamato-daiwa/backend";

        Server.initializeAndStart({
          IP_Address: "127.0.0.1",
          HTTP: { port: ProtocolDependentDefaultPorts.HTTP },
          routing: [

            {
              HTTP_Method: HTTP_Methods.get,
              pathTemplate: "products/:PRODUCT_ID",
              async handler(request: Request, response: Response): Promise<void> {

                const targetProductID: number = request.validateAndProcessRoutePathParameters<{ PRODUCT_ID: number; }>({
                  PRODUCT_ID: {
                    preValidationModifications: convertPotentialStringToIntegerIfPossible,
                    type: Number,
                    numbersSet: RawObjectDataProcessor.NumbersSets.naturalNumberOrZero,
                    isUndefinedForbidden: true,
                    isNullForbidden: true
                  }
                }).PRODUCT_ID;

                return response.submitWithSuccess({
                  HTML_Content: `<h1>Product with ID: ${ targetProductID }</h1>`
                });

              }
            },

            // ...

          ]
        });

    +QuestionAndAnswerBox--YDF({
      type: QuestionAndAnswerBox__YDF.Types.criticism,
      question: "Code got more. The Express.js version is simpler and cleaner."
    }).
      First, the above examples are #[+Keyword--YDID no]&nbsp;equivalent because #[+SecondaryEntity--YDID Express.js]
        version has #[+Keyword--YDID no] validation of #[+Term--YDID route parameters].
      Second, saying this you are #[+Keyword--YDID focused] one write the initial code ASAP, not on code
        maintainability or minimizing of a logic error probability.
      The low code #[+Keyword--YDID not]&nbsp;always gives the good maintainability, and sometimes these two
        characteristics are conflicting.
      "Much code" itself is #[+Keyword--YDID neither] good #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID nor] bad, here is important
        does large volume of the code justified, or actually can be reduces without maintainability impact.
      #[+Keyphrase--YDID Is out case], there is such #[+Keyword--YDID justification].

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      Because the specifying of validation rules is #[+Keyword--YDID required] when calling
        #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID validateAndProcessRoutePathParameters] #[+Term--YDID method],
        this #[+Term--YDID method] #[+Keyword--YDID forces] the programmer to reinforce the casting to
        #[+Term--YDID type] passed as #[+Term--YDID generic parameter] by the validation.
      Of course, because of considered above #[+ImportantEntity--YDID TypeScript] limitations specified validation
        rules may mismatch with the #[+Term--YDID type] passed as #[+Term--YDID generic parameter], but it is better
        than just type casting and, as practice shows, even with such limitations the mistake probability will be
        #[+Keyword--YDID significantly] #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID less].
      Later in this lesson we will improve this code and, this way, reduce the mistake probability again.


    //- ─── Numeric Path Parameters ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    +Heading--YDF({
      level: 4,
      text: localizedSectioning.routingInYDB.$children.pathParameters.$children.numeric.$heading,
      HTML_ID: localizedSectioning.routingInYDB.$children.pathParameters.$children.numeric.$anchor
    }).Article-Heading4

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      #[+Keyphrase--YDID At first sight] it is obvious that #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID 1] in
        #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID https://example.com/products/1] is the #[+Term--YDID number].
      However a #[+Keyword--YDID majority] of frameworks (herewith not&nbsp;only #[+ImportantEntity--YDID Node.js] ones)
        parses the #[+Term--YDID route path parameter] as #[+Keyword--YDID string] by default
        (it #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID natural] because #[+ImportantEntity--YDID URI] itself is a #[+Keyword--YDID string],
        and trying to parse #[+Keyword--YDID each] #[+Term--YDID route parameter] for #[+Keyword--YDID all]
        #[+ImportantEntity--YDID URI]s is the performance impact, even if not large impact.
      In this way, when submitting of th request to #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID /products/:PRODUCT_ID]
        #[+Term--YDID route] we can specify not&nbsp;only #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID 1], but also
        #[+Keyword--YDID arbitrary] #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID combination] of letters and digits.

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      Nevertheless, sometimes we are actually need the specific #[+Term--YDID route path parameters] be the
        #[+Term--YDID numbers].
      Particularly, for the storing of the entities in database, numeric #[+Term--YDID keys] are frequently being used,
        moreover, usually the are #[+Keyword--YDID non-negative] #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID integers].
      (Although when building the #[+Term--YDID SQL request] everything will be turned to #[+Term--YDID string],
        the respective of numeric type of #[+Term--YDID route path parameters] is still required is some cases,
        and also, #[+Keyword--YDID not]&nbsp;all #[+Term--YDID database management system] are useing the
        #[+ImportantEntity--YDID SQL]).
      By other words, suc #[+ImportantEntity--YDID URI]s as
        #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID http://127.0.0.1/products/1.2] or
        #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID http://127.0.0.1/products/-3] will be considered as #[+Keyword--YDID invalid]
        by us no matter it contains the number.

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      Although in the frameworks like #[+SecondaryEntity--YDID Express.js] when defining the
        #[+Term--YDID route path parameter] we can use the #[+Term--YDID regular expressions] accepting only
        #[+Term--YDID numbers], without third-party libraries we are still need to transform this #[+Term--YDID parameter]
        to numeric #[+Term--YDID data type]:

    +CodeViewer--YDF
      +CodeViewer-TabPanel--YDF({ codeLanguage: CodeViewer__YDF.SupportedCodesLanguages.TypeScript })

        +CodeViewer-PartialListing--YDF: :code_listing--yda
          import type { Express as ExpressApplication } from "express";
          import type Express from "express";
          import createExpressApplication from "express";


          const expressApplication: ExpressApplication = createExpressApplication();

          expressApplication.get(
            "/products/:ID(\\d+)",
            (request: Express.Request<{ ID: string; }>, response: Express.Response): void => {
              console.log(typeof request.params.ID);
        +CodeViewer-Explanation--YDF.
          Despite the regular expression guarantees only numeric characters, the console output will be
            #[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID string].
          Before do anything with it #[+Keyphrase--YDID as with number], wee need to transform it to numeric
            #[+Term--YDID type] by #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID Number()] or
            #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID parseInt], if we need the #[+Keyword--YDID integer] #[+Term--YDID type].
        +CodeViewer-PartialListing--YDF: :code_listing--yda
              response.send(`<h1>Product with ID: ${ request.params.ID }</h1>`);
            }
          );

          expressApplication.listen(80, "127.0.0.1");

    p.Article-Paragraph.
      In #[+ImportantEntity--YDID YDB] case, as it obvious from
        #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID validateAndProcessRoutePathParameters] #[+Term--YDID method] name,
        this method not&nbsp;only validated the #[+Term--YDID route path parameters], but only cas process them,
        in particular transform them to numeric type
      It is simple, but needs the detailed explanation.

    ul.Article-UnorderedList

      li.
        For transformation to #[+Keyword--YDID numeric] #[+Term--YDID type] (let repeat that #[+Keyword--YDID initially])
          #[+Keyword--YDID all] #[+Term--YDID route path parameters] are #[+Term--YDID strings] and transforming to
          #[+Term--YDID numbers] is #[+Keyword--YDID on-demand]), it is required to use the functionality which called
          "pre validation modification" in #[+ImportantEntity--YDID RawObjectDataProcessor]'s #[+Term--YDID API].
        In the essence it is the #[+Term--YDID function] #[+Keyword--YDID or] #[+Term--YDID array] of the
          #[+Term--YDID functions], which accepts #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID unknown]-type #[+Term--YDID parameter]
          and returns it's value either transformed or as is, in dependence of the targets for which functionality
          being used.
        In our case it is required to transform the #[+Keyword--YDID stringified] value to #[+Keyword--YDID number]
          #[+Keyphrase--YDID only if it contains the valid number].
        For such cases, there is the #[+Keyword--YDID pre-made] #[+Term--YDID function]
          #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID convertPotentialStringToNumberIfPossible].
        It also possible use the #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID convertPotentialStringToIntegerIfPossible]
          #[+Term--YDID function], but if the #[+Term--YDID string] will contain the #[+Keyword--YDID non-integer]
          #[+Term--YDID number], it will&nbsp;#[+Keyword--YDID not]&nbsp;transformed to number and the validation
          error message may confuse.

      li.
        Now, when the #[+Term--YDID route parameter] has been successfully transformed to #[+Term--YDID number] (if
          it was possible), the #[+Keyword--YDID expected] type specification #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID type: String]
          #[+Keyword--YDID must] be replaced with #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID type: Number].

      li.
        According to #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID RawObjectDataProcessor]'s  #[+Term--YDID API], if
          #[+Term--YDID number] is the expected type than it is #[+Keyword--YDID required] to specify the
          #[+Term--YDID number set] #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID numbersSet] #[+Term--YDID property], and also
          the #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID NaN] prohibition.
        If you are expecting #[+Keyword--YDID only] #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID positive] #[+Keyword--YDID integers],
          specify #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID RawObjectDataProcessor.NumbersSets.naturalNumberOrZero],
          and if you are want to forbid #[+ImportantEntity--YDID 0], it will be the
          #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID RawObjectDataProcessor.NumbersSets.naturalNumber].
        Also, there is #[+Keyword--YDID no]&nbsp;need to allow #[+ImportantEntity--YDID NaN].

    +CodeViewer--YDF
      +CodeViewer-Listing--YDF({ codeLanguage: CodeViewer__YDF.SupportedCodesLanguages.TypeScript }): :code_listing--yda
        import { Server, Request, Response, ProtocolDependentDefaultPorts } from "@yamato-daiwa/backend";
        import {
          HTTP_Methods,
          convertPotentialStringToNumberIfPossible,
          RawObjectDataProcessor
        } from "@yamato-daiwa/es-extensions";


        Server.initializeAndStart({
          IP_Address: "127.0.0.1",
          HTTP: { port: ProtocolDependentDefaultPorts.HTTP },
